Transient mild heartburn related to late-night eating, highly responsive to over-the-counter antacids
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Retrosternal burning, Exacerbation when supine, Rapid antacid relief

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-046
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of retrosternal burning and chest discomfort requiring immediate assessment to rule out acute coronary syndrome or myocardial ischemia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden, unremitting central chest burning that radiated towards the neck. Urgent physical assessment and electrocardiogram were strictly required prior to the next in-hours period to rule out an atypical presentation of acute myocardial infarction, which carries an immediate threat to life if misdiagnosed as simple reflux.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My chest is burning right behind my breastbone and it gets worse if I lie down.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a retrosternal burning sensation radiating upwards toward the neck, beginning shortly after eating a heavy meal late at night. Reports worsening of burning upon lying completely flat. Notes rapid subjective relief within minutes of ingesting basic over-the-counter antacids. Complete absence of crushing chest pressure, shortness of breath, or diaphoresis. Denies dysphagia, sensation of food getting stuck, or hematemesis. Intact baseline vocal quality without chronic hoarseness.
Functional Status: Fully independent, sitting upright to avoid pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding a potential heart attack.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, no signs of acute cardiopulmonary distress, occasionally rubbing central chest.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular examination reveals normal heart sounds (S1, S2) with no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Normal resting electrocardiogram ruling out acute cardiac ischemia. Diagnostic triad confirmed: retrosternal burning, exacerbation when supine, and rapid antacid relief. Chest wall is non-tender to palpation. Abdomen is soft and non-tender in the epigastrium. Clear lung fields bilaterally.
Environmental Observations: Empty antacid wrappers and remnants of a large, late meal visible.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient not taking regular proton pump inhibitors.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Transient mild heartburn related to late-night eating, highly responsive to over-the-counter antacids - Status: Improving
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal resting ECG, absence of exertional triggers, and clear, immediate relief with antacid administration.

Peptic Ulcer Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of gnawing epigastric pain that wakes the patient from sleep, and lack of melena or hematemesis.

Esophageal Spasm - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of dysphagia or severe squeezing pain radiating to the back.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute central chest pain, a presentation that mandates immediate exclusion of acute coronary syndrome. Delaying the assessment until regular clinic hours posed an unacceptable risk of missing a life-threatening cardiac event. The urgent home evaluation included a thorough cardiovascular examination and ECG, confirming normal cardiac function and explicitly linking the symptoms to gastric acid reflux. This safely averted a costly emergency department transfer while providing necessary reassurance and symptom relief.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance following normal cardiac assessment. Recommendation for an over-the-counter liquid alginate-antacid combination to provide a physical barrier against reflux.
Positive Behavioral Change: Elevate the head of the bed by placing books under the mattress, allowing gravity to physically prevent stomach acid from traveling up the esophagus during sleep.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bed wedge pillow.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if heartburn occurs more than twice a week.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop heavy, crushing chest pain, pain radiating to your left arm or jaw, shortness of breath, or if you begin vomiting blood.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical gastroesophageal reflux; atypical cardiac events can occasionally mimic these symptoms.
Clinical Course: The burning sensation should fully resolve within an hour of taking antacids and remaining upright.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you feel like food is getting stuck in your throat, or if you suddenly lose weight without trying.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A heavy meal late at night filled your stomach, and lying flat allowed the acidic contents to spill backward into your esophagus. The esophagus does not have a protective lining against acid, causing the burning pain.
Actionable Steps: Do not eat within 3 hours of going to bed. Sleep with your head elevated. Take the liquid antacid immediately before sleeping.

SMS INFORMATION

